{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And you see among the captives, etc.</b> It uses 'among the captives' to denote abundance — for what business does a woman have in war? She was surely brought there for purposes of licentiousness, and therefore it would have been fitting for her to be abhorrent to him, for the lot of the wicked should not rest upon the portion of the righteous. And if your urge presses you in any case — 'and you desire her' — then 'you shall take her for yourself as a wife.' The word 'for yourself' is superfluous: although our Rabbis of blessed memory derived (Kiddushin 22a) 'for yourself' and not for his father or his son, nevertheless we may explain according to the plain sense that 'for yourself' means and not for your seed — for this imagined benefit is only for you, but for your seed, on the contrary, the matter is harmful, for in the end you will have from her children who are worthless, and that is the wayward and rebellious son. 'And you shall bring her into the interior of your house' — that is, a room within a room, a covering for the eyes, for your shame, so that your evil not be revealed in the congregation." "<b>And she shall shave her head and do her nails, etc.</b> The Ramban explained that all of these are matters of mourning. And the principal device is so that he should be in a house of mourning and remember his urge on the day of death, in the manner that our Rabbis of blessed memory said (there, 40a): one who sees that his urge is overpowering him should don black garments and wrap himself in black garments, etc. — and perhaps his urge will depart through the remembrance of mourning. And it says here that if despite all these things he is not restrained and still stands in the passion of his desire, then 'you may come to her and be her husband, and she shall be your wife' — 'for yourself' alone she is fitting and you are fitting for her, for she is like you." "<b>And it shall be, if you do not desire her.</b> Scripture informs you that your end will be to hate her. The word 'and it shall be' — some say it indicates certainty, that thus it will surely be. And according to our approach, this is most certainly inferred from the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory, that through remembrance of the day of death the evil urge surely departs, and then automatically the passion ceases. And it seems likely that Rashi was precise with the word 'חָפַצְתָּ' — past tense — for it should have said 'תַחְפֹּץ' in the future; but anything that is revealed before HaShem blessed be He as something that will be, is as though it were already done from of old and from aforetime — therefore it says: 'if you did not desire her.' And it is possible further to explain the word 'and it shall be' as implying that the matter will endure in its abiding state and will not change; and in this Scripture informs you that once you began to cast hatred upon her, so it shall be forever, and your disposition will not change from hatred to love. And this is what it says — that your end will be to hate her, and this is from HaShem — so as not to mix the grapes of the vine with the grapes of the thorn-bush. And this is a correct inference from the word 'and it shall be,' and it also settles well the word 'if' which indicates uncertainty, and according to our words it is well settled."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "The Talmudic citation 'שם מ:' refers to Kiddushin 40a, the same tractate as the earlier citation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:10.899718150Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}